      {
        "id": "char_1",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "FTIR",
        "is_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "FTIR shows broad peak at 3300-3500 cm⁻¹ for surface hydroxyl groups of nano-TiO₂, red-shifted in MNT due to hydroxyl to amino transformation, new peaks at 2923, 2854 cm⁻¹ for CH₂, 1560 cm⁻¹ for NH₂, and 1120 cm⁻¹ for Si-O-Ti confirming APTES grafting.",
        "evidence_snippet": "Figure 1b. shows the FTIR spectra of the nano-TiO₂ and MNT. In the spectrum of nano-TiO₂, the broad peak within the range of 3300 - 3500 cm⁻¹ is attributed to the stretching vibration of hydroxyl groups on its surface."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_2",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "XPS",
        "is_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "XPS of MNT shows new N 1s and Si 2p peaks; high-resolution Si 2p peak at 102.13 eV confirms APTES attachment (2.6 at% Si); N 1s peaks at 398.05, 399.29, and 401.23 eV attributed to terminal NH₂, hydrogen-bonded NH₂/NH₃⁺.",
        "evidence_snippet": "The sharp peak at 102.13 eV in the Si 2p spectrum provides definitive evidence of APTES attachment to the nano-TiO₂ surface, and the surface composition analysis reveals that the MNT contains 2.6 at% silicon. Three distinct N 1s peaks were observed at 398.05, 399.29, and 401.23 eV."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_3",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "TGA",
        "is_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "TGA shows nano-TiO₂ has 1.42% mass loss at 300-800°C; MNT shows 3.56% mass loss in same range due to APTES decomposition, estimating 2.14 wt% APTES moiety.",
        "evidence_snippet": "Figure S2, Supporting Information shows the TGA curves of nano-TiO₂ and MNT. ... MNT shows a mass loss of 3.56% within the same temperature range, which should be caused by the thermal decomposition of APTES moiety on its surface."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_4",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "XRD",
        "is_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "XRD patterns of nano-TiO₂ and MNT show similar diffraction peaks, proving APTES modification did not affect the crystal type of nano-TiO₂.",
        "evidence_snippet": "Figure S3, Supporting Information presents the XRD patterns of nano-TiO₂ and MNT. Their diffraction peaks are similar, which proves that the modification by APTES did not affect the crystal type of nano-TiO₂."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_5",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "Zeta Potential",
        "is_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "Zeta potential shows isoelectric point of MNT at 8.4 (higher than nano-TiO₂ at 6.8); MNT shows more positive charges below pH 7 enhancing colloidal stability via electrostatic repulsion.",
        "evidence_snippet": "The zeta potentials of nano-TiO₂ and MNT in the aqueous dispersion of various pH values are given in Figure 1d. It can be seen that the isoelectric point (8.4) of MNT is higher than that (6.8) of nano-TiO₂."
      }
